export default "This is a comparison between two apps that made the same core decision: keep period data away from a readable company database. No account is needed for core tracking. The differences between Floriva and Euki are about everything else.\n\n## The Core Difference\n\nBoth Floriva and Euki are [zero-knowledge period trackers](/resources/guides/what-is-zero-knowledge-period-tracker). Neither company can access your data because neither company stores it. That puts them in a different category from cloud-based apps like Flo, Clue, or Natural Cycles.\n\nThe distinction between them comes down to funding model, platform maturity, and what happens when you have more than one device.\n\n## Data Architecture\n\n**Floriva** stores core cycle data in your device's local storage. No account is needed for core tracking. Optional sync works through end-to-end encrypted transfer. Your data moves between your devices without giving Floriva readable records.\n\n**Euki** takes the same local-only approach. All data lives on the device. No account. No cloud component. Euki adds a decoy screen: if someone picks up your phone and opens the app, you can configure it to show made-up content instead of your real data.\n\nBoth apps reduce what the company can produce. Euki has no readable central records. Floriva has no readable central cycle database. Device access is a separate legal and physical risk.\n\nThe meaningful difference is sync. Floriva lets you keep data consistent across devices through encrypted transfer. Euki does not sync at all. Your data lives on exactly one device.\n\n## Features\n\nEuki was built as a sexual and reproductive health tool, not just a period tracker. It includes content on contraception, abortion, STIs, and pregnancy alongside cycle tracking. This broader scope reflects its nonprofit mission through Women Help Women.\n\nFloriva is focused on cycle tracking with symptom logging, predictions, and pattern analysis. It aims to be a dedicated tracking tool rather than a health resource platform.\n\nBoth apps track periods, symptoms, and fertility indicators. Neither app uses your data for advertising, analytics, or research, because neither app has access to your data.\n\nEuki's decoy screen is a feature Floriva does not match. If physical device privacy matters, a partner, parent, or authority figure might open your phone. The decoy screen provides a layer of protection that software architecture alone does not address.\n\nFloriva's encrypted sync is a feature Euki does not match. If you switch phones, get a new device, or use both iOS and Android, Floriva preserves your data across that transition. With Euki, a lost or broken phone means lost data.\n\n## Pricing\n\n**Euki:** Free. Funded by Women Help Women. No premium tier, no ads, no in-app purchases. The risk with grant-funded apps is that funding can change. If Women Help Women shifts priorities, Euki's development could slow or stop.\n\n**Floriva:** Paid subscription, no free tier. The subscription funds ongoing development without dependency on outside organizations. The trade-off is straightforward: you pay for the app.\n\n## Who Should Choose Floriva vs Euki\n\n**Choose Euki if:** you want a free, on-device tracker with broader sexual health resources, you value the decoy screen for physical privacy, or you are on a single device and do not need sync. Euki is the right choice for anyone who needs privacy without a subscription.\n\n**Choose Floriva if:** you use multiple devices, you want active development with regular updates, or you prefer a subscription model over grant-dependent funding. Floriva is the right choice if optional encrypted sync matters and you are willing to pay for it.\n\nBoth are strong choices. The [best private period tracker apps](/resources/best/best-private-period-tracker-apps) list includes both. They represent two workable models for a period tracker that cannot leak your data.\n\n## The Privacy Bottom Line\n\nBoth apps pass the basic privacy test. Euki has no company server records. Floriva has no readable central cycle database. That limits what can be handed to law enforcement, advertisers, or data brokers.\n\nThe decision comes down to secondary factors: cost, sync, physical device privacy, development pace, and platform support. That is a better set of trade-offs than choosing between private and convenient, which is what cloud-based tracker comparisons usually come down to.";
